00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.269
We realized that technology wasn't

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:16.449
enhancing what we were doing,

00:11:16.450 --> 00:11:18.869
technology was holding us back.

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:20.249
And so we asked ourselves, is there

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:21.429
a way where we could utilize

00:11:21.430 --> 00:11:23.249
technology to build the

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:24.389
best real estate investment

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:26.389
management platform and further

00:11:26.390 --> 00:11:27.849
our mission to be the real estate

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:29.309
partner of choice?

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:30.809
We quickly realized to do that, we

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.629
had to get our employees out from

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.229
behind computer screens.

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:35.969
And out in front of our partners and

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:36.970
our tenants.

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.699
When we decided where we were gonna

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:42.719
engage with Palantir, we

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.199
knew it had to be somewhere where we

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.059
could improve a workflow

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:47.619
that would make a real immediate

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:48.620
difference.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.099
And so we chose accounting.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.379
I know accounting isn't the most

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.559
exciting workflow

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:55.999
you could choose, but I knew if we

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:57.119
could nail accounting, we could

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:58.239
probably literally do anything.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.699
And so, we decided to dig in.

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.099
And it's funny, when we dug in, we

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:04.539
realized it wasn't just our

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:05.719
accountants in corporate offices

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:07.229
doing accounting work.

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:08.849
Accounting was literally taking time

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:09.850
for everybody,

00:12:10.829 --> 00:12:12.409
including the people on the front

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:14.089
lines, our property managers.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:15.929
Our property managers live at the

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:17.309
properties. That's where they do

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.529
their day to day.

00:12:18.530 --> 00:12:20.209
And instead of being out serving

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:21.649
those clinicians, serving our

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:23.369
tenants, they were stuck behind

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:25.369
computer screens doing accounting.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:26.869
And so we realized that was

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:28.529
something we had to fix.

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:29.489
We've gone through a lot of

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:30.589
different processes that we've

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:31.749
improved, but I wanna give you one

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:33.749
example of something that

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:36.099
we improved just recently.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:37.479
Every single month at Healthpeak,

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:39.679
across our 700 properties,

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:41.779
we have thousands of sub-meters

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:43.779
that are generating data for

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:45.839
us, recording how much

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.439
energy utilization is going on in

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:48.339
each suite.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.099
And every single month, our property

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.499
managers have to take that data,

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:53.699
and they have to go take pictures of

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:55.019
it, and they to upload it into a

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:55.879
spreadsheet, and they do

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:57.559
calculations, they have send it to

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:59.160
accounting, they have comments, it

00:12:59.161 --> 00:13:01.019
comes back, it takes hours

00:13:01.020 --> 00:13:02.659
and hours of our property manager's

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:03.899
time. It takes them away from our

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.059
tenants and puts them behind a

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.060
computer screen.

00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:08.079
We knew we needed to figure out how

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:09.080
to fix it.

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:11.959
So I want to go just a little behind

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.019
the scenes and show you what we

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.020
built.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:16.919
When we ultimately stepped back, we

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:18.499
knew all you really needed to do was

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.299
take a picture of a submeter.

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:21.919
And if we could get that submeter

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.739
picture and some information,

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.339
we could do the whole process

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:26.799
automatically.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:27.919
We didn't need all these people

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:28.999
doing all these things, sending

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:30.239
spreadsheets back and forth, doing

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:31.539
calculations.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.979
We could have AIP and our ontology

00:13:33.980 --> 00:13:36.499
do the entire process,

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:38.259
obviously flagging instances where

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:40.459
there are issues or concerns.

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:42.299
I want to jump into one of them

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:44.219
where we notice an issue.

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:45.559
You can see this is basically just a

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:47.399
picture of a sub-meter, it's a

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.379
utility bill, and a lot of

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:51.479
different items that

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:53.719
the AIP is checking regarding

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:55.059
the matching of those two items.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:57.379
The system knows everything.

00:13:57.380 --> 00:13:59.339
It knows how to apply the correct

00:13:59.340 --> 00:14:00.219
methodology.

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:01.719
It knows to get the correct dates

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:02.719
and rates.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.339
It calculates the tenant's share of

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:06.319
the expense. It creates the invoice.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:07.879
It runs it through accounting.

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:09.419
It compares it to historical and

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:10.579
projected amounts.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.179
And ultimately it saves us a ton of

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.079
time because instead of a property

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:15.679
manager going through all these

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:17.559
individual items, they're

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:18.739
only looking at the things that were

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:20.359
flagged. So they can get out from

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:21.679
behind the desk and back in front of

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:22.680
our tenants.

00:14:24.609 --> 00:14:26.529
That's been super exciting.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.069
We're very excited about what we're

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:29.249
building in accounting, but

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:30.529
honestly, we're more excited about

00:14:30.530 --> 00:14:31.909
what were doing in 2026.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:34.869
This is where I think the platform

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:36.369
can really come to life.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.269
Accounting was a building block.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:39.689
It provided the foundation of what

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.229
we were trying to build, but the

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:42.789
next step is to really create an

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:43.909
operating system that connects

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:45.349
everything in our company.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:47.729
Our people, our buildings, our data.

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:49.289
And to do that, we knew one of the

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:51.189
things that we would have to do was

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:52.629
GoMobile.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:54.329
We decided that we needed to build

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:56.349
an app to give our property managers

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:58.289
and our team access to

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:00.049
our ontology and our operating

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:01.849
system wherever they are, whenever

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:02.949
they need it.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:04.309
I wanna walk you through one example

00:15:04.310 --> 00:15:05.310
of something we're building.

00:15:07.690 --> 00:15:09.049
Every day across all of our

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:10.050
buildings,

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:12.849
there's thousands and thousands of

00:15:12.850 --> 00:15:14.609
interactions happening.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:15.929
Our tenants are speaking with our

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:16.930
property managers.

00:15:18.230 --> 00:15:19.649
They're having conversations about

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:21.009
the quality of the space, about what

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:22.009
they're trying to do.

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:23.909
Sometimes it's complaints.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:25.929
And we ask ourselves, how do we

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.989
capture all of that information?

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.089
And so what we have here is

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.889
basically our property manager going

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:32.749
into a conversation and

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:34.489
saying, hey, I just talked to the

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:35.309
tenant.

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:36.169
They have a big need.

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:37.959
They need some new people.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.279
They're hiring a lot of people, and

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:40.539
by the way, it's pretty hot in their

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:42.379
office, we might need to adjust the

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:43.380
AC.

00:15:43.680 --> 00:15:45.579
At that moment, our property manager

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:47.139
doesn't have to go into some legacy

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.239
CRM system, they don't have jotted

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:50.379
down in a notebook, they can

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:51.939
literally just grab their phone,

00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:53.839
record a message, and it starts

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:55.479
a chain reaction.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:15:57.539
This is what we're excited about.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.099
So at that moment after they record

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:00.739
it, it connects all the dots, and it

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:02.499
not only records the note, it

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:03.500
activates the process.

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:07.959
The first one is to our leasing

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:09.259
team.

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:11.099
We're not waiting for our tenants to

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:12.899
tell us they need more space.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:15.279
The system is actively monitoring

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:17.119
our ontology, looking for

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.099
key instances that could indicate

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:21.079
a tenant and our partners need more

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:22.299
place.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.239
In this case, more employees means

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.299
more space, the system

00:16:26.300 --> 00:16:27.199
then looks at their current

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.159
footprint, it analyzes their growth

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:30.259
pattern, it looks at what's

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.199
available in our buildings and

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.179
surfaces logical next steps that are

00:16:34.180 --> 00:16:35.369
fed to the team.

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:36.730
That they can then go act on.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:39.809
Second one was for the facilities, a

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:40.909
separate action.

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:42.709
The room was hot, notwithstanding

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.329
the thermostat being wrong.

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:45.789
The system can look at the HVAC

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:47.189
history for that suite.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:48.669
It can identify whether there's been

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:50.529
similar issues in the past, and it

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:52.529
can read the information to

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:54.269
determine the likely root cause and

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:55.189
next step.

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:57.149
For us, that's real leverage.

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:58.489
It's the best people in the best

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:00.489
buildings now supported by a system

00:17:00.490 --> 00:17:02.449
that amplifies their impact and the

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:03.450
performance.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:08.019
Siding part now, we

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:09.179
have the information.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:10.899
Our engineers diagnose the issues,

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:12.439
we can use our operating system to

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:14.019
create real leverage.

00:17:14.020 --> 00:17:15.799
Not only can we look at that unit or

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:17.699
that HVAC unit, we can look at

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:19.118
every maintenance ticket across our

00:17:19.119 --> 00:17:20.439
company. We can look energy

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:22.358
consumption data, the age and

00:17:22.359 --> 00:17:24.039
warranty status of the equipment.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:25.358
And critically, cause going back to

00:17:25.359 --> 00:17:26.818
the accounting piece, now we can all

00:17:26.819 --> 00:17:29.239
the costs we've incurred over time.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:30.479
We're able to then leverage our

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.359
entire scale and start making

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:33.899
capital allocation decisions

00:17:33.900 --> 00:17:35.219
informed by operational

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:36.509
intelligence.

00:17:36.510 --> 00:17:38.309
We can create a future where every

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:40.029
repair, every maintenance, every

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:41.309
dollar spent across our entire

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.789
portfolio can be informed,

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:45.209
and it can help us deploy our

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:46.210
capital better.

00:17:48.830 --> 00:17:51.229
The kind of operating model

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:53.029
that we're building toward is

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:53.889
unique.

00:17:53.890 --> 00:17:55.409
It's a platform that will learn from

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.369
every interaction, and it raises the

00:17:57.370 --> 00:17:58.429
performance of everything we're

00:17:58.430 --> 00:18:00.169
doing, and ultimately gets back to

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.449
our goal of delivering the best

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:03.689
tenant experience for our partners.

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:05.729
Through the automation, the nuance

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.169
captured in this one phone call,

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:09.109
this one interaction, generates a

00:18:09.110 --> 00:18:10.549
series of activities that they're

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:12.309
acted upon. We're not waiting

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.169
anymore for somebody to jot it

00:18:14.170 --> 00:18:16.329
down and to make sure it gets done.

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:18.529
One simple conversation driving real

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:19.849
measurable results across the

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:21.289
organization.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:22.789
As our portfolio grows, we're no

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:24.089
longer limited by the number of

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.489
people we have.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:26.649
We're now just limited by, we're not

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:28.289
even limited by the technology.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:29.809
We can grow our portfolio without

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:31.309
adding any incremental costs, and

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:32.490
for us, that's very big.

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:35.989
We're excited to have shown

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:36.889
this to you guys today.

00:18:36.890 --> 00:18:38.449
We're about the transformation we're

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:39.729
making at HealthBeak.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:41.369
We have an installation there in the

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:42.589
back. We'd invite you to come join

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:43.689
us and check it out when we're done.

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:44.690
Thank you.

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:50.459
Please welcome Senior Vice President

00:18:50.460 --> 00:18:52.499
and Chief Transformation Officer at

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:54.359
Tampa General Hospital,

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.360
Dr. Peter Chang.

00:19:02.910 --> 00:19:04.009
Good evening, everyone.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:05.329
Thanks for having me here tonight.

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:06.969
So excited to be here in this

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:08.449
amazing venue.

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:10.209
Thank you, Alex, for putting all of

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:11.249
this together.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.169
Now, let's set the stage of

00:19:13.170 --> 00:19:15.129
what healthcare is, at least

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:16.189
for Tampa General.

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:19.309
We are a $4.5 billion organization

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.289
in West Central Florida,

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:22.889
so lots of great revenue, but we

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:25.089
operate in the hospital at

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:27.169
around a one to a 1.5%

00:19:27.170 --> 00:19:28.170
margin.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:30.769
So if we have a 2% Medicare cut

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:32.639
this year, Guess what happens

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.459
when Medicare is about 40%

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:36.339
of our payer base.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:37.879
Every year the government has

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:39.059
reductions on what they're

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:40.579
reimbursing healthcare.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:42.139
Insurance companies are finding

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:44.059
cruel and unusual ways to pay us

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:45.679
less money.

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:47.539
Labor's going up and with

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:49.339
tariffs, supplies and equipment is

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:50.259
going up.

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.059
So the only way that we can work

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:54.099
ourselves out of this situation is

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:55.359
to innovate.

00:19:55.360 --> 00:19:56.959
And I'll kind of ask a question out

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:57.779
to the audience.

00:19:57.780 --> 00:19:59.619
What are the two last industries

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:01.449
to innovate?

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:03.729
I'll give you one, is healthcare.

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:05.489
And the second is education, right?

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:06.669
A teacher will get up in front of a

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:08.509
classroom and teach one way, but

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:10.009
each student may learn in a

00:20:10.010 --> 00:20:12.069
different way, and there's no way to

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:13.669
customize how you're gonna teach a

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:15.689
student, and I translate the same

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:17.149
thing over to medicine.

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:18.989
There's no that you could take two

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:20.529
diabetic patients with the same

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:22.569
numbers and treat them exactly

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:23.829
the same way.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:26.719
So the mantra of pathway-based care.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.419
Is interesting, and I think is

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:29.879
evidence-based, but at the same

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:31.679
point, we need to find a way to

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.719
customize care for our patients, and

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:34.779
that's what I'm gonna show you later

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:35.780
today.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:38.699
So what we have here are the three

00:20:38.700 --> 00:20:40.339
big pillars that we look at within

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:41.579
our health system.

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:43.159
We're trying to find the way to

00:20:43.160 --> 00:20:46.599
weave together clinical side,

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:48.379
operational side, and finance,

00:20:48.380 --> 00:20:49.799
because the finance is so important

00:20:49.800 --> 00:20:51.839
to us, and the connective tissue

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:53.879
that weaves through all of that.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.079
Today, we're going to focus in on

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:56.619
the care navigation administrative

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:58.599
view and take you through a

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.459
patient journey of a

00:21:00.460 --> 00:21:02.139
patient by the name of Jane that

00:21:02.140 --> 00:21:03.559
unfortunately got admitted to the

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:04.859
ICU.

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:06.599
Now, if you've ever been in an

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:08.659
hospital before, you realize

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:10.499
that you give

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:12.579
total control away.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:13.939
That's the number one thing you lose

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:15.099
in a hospital.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:17.079
And so what you're seeing now is a

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:18.619
live feed. Think about almost like

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:20.599
an ex-feed for a physician.

00:21:20.600 --> 00:21:21.679
So as things are happening

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:23.229
throughout the hospital, you're

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:24.629
getting notifications of what's

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:26.029
going on, the information is coming

00:21:26.030 --> 00:21:28.109
to find you versus me having

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.089
to go back into our EMR and check on

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:30.989
data.

00:21:30.990 --> 00:21:32.809
So Jane's had a CT of her

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:34.349
chest, a CAT scan, she's had blood

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:36.329
cultures, she's diagnosed with

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:37.209
sepsis.

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:39.189
Oh, by the way, we use a Palantir

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:41.029
algorithm to pick sepsus up

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:42.869
earlier and get a patient on a

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:43.870
treatment pathway.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:46.489
What does it result in?

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:48.809
Is a 68% reduction

00:21:48.810 --> 00:21:50.799
in 48-hour mortality.

00:21:50.800 --> 00:21:52.679
So within two days, is this patient

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.219
still with us or not?

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:55.579
In the last two years, that's

00:21:55.580 --> 00:21:57.899
resulted in 700 additional patients

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.659
being able to leave the hospital

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:02.299
versus how we operated before.

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:03.719
Hospitals around the country get

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.699
sepsis right about 20 to 30%

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:06.579
of the time.

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:07.839
So how would you like to be a

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:09.999
patient walking into that scenario?

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:12.139
So in this example, we're actually

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:13.959
walking through where a

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:16.479
pulmonology clearance is given,

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:18.079
a ID clearance is giving.

00:22:18.080 --> 00:22:20.099
The patient meets downgrade criteria

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:22.839
out of the ICU because they've

00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:24.939
responded well to the antibiotics.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:26.839
We know that by the feed over on

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:29.039
the side and the patient's

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:30.639
getting ready to move on to the next

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:31.919
stage of the care.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:34.019
You have an AIP

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.079
reader that's looking over the notes

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:37.939
and actually suggesting the next

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:39.759
best pathway, which is to

00:22:39.760 --> 00:22:41.440
downgrade the patient from the ICU.

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:44.639
As we start moving on.

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:46.439
We'll make the recommendation that

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.999
now the patient can be downgraded

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:49.859
out of the ICU, but we have a

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:51.559
problem here because now the

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:54.179
patients still needs IV antibiotics

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:56.099
and that IV antibiotic course

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:57.739
is gonna be an additional four days

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.619
in the hospital outside of

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:00.759
the ICU.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:02.719
So it adds an additional 4 days to

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:04.299
our length of stay, taking a bed

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.199
away from another patient.

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:08.019
Luckily, we have AIP

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:09.819
reading the chart and recommending

00:23:09.820 --> 00:23:11.339
hospital at home.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:13.339
So what that is, is the ability to

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:15.279
take the hospital-based care and

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:16.999
actually take it out and mobilize it

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:19.099
to the patient's home and

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:20.659
get the patient moving out of the

00:23:20.660 --> 00:23:22.539
hospital. So it's running an

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:24.199
eligibility in the background that

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:25.979
says consider hospital at home.

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:27.379
So as a physician, I don't need to

00:23:27.380 --> 00:23:29.059
remember does this patient live

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:31.139
close enough to the hospital?

00:23:31.140 --> 00:23:32.019
What is their payer?

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:33.459
Because only certain payers cover

00:23:33.460 --> 00:23:34.819
hospital at home at this point in

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:36.779
time. And then again, being able

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:38.939
to enroll that patient into

00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:41.329
the hospital at-home program easily.

00:23:41.330 --> 00:23:42.989
So I can go ahead and look at my

00:23:42.990 --> 00:23:43.949
criteria.

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:45.689
I can see my milestones have been

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:47.789
met for IV antibiotics,

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:49.569
downgrade out of the ICU, where the

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:51.649
patient lives, what their payer is,

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:53.649
and I'm moving on to the next

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:55.709
step, which is getting this patient

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:57.269
enrolled into the Hospital at Home

00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:58.329
program.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:00.169
So as that happens, you'll

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:02.449
see AIP is helping out,

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:04.349
create the referral order, get that

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:05.989
placed out to that team so they can

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:07.929
get ready to enroll that patient

00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:09.849
next into the system.

00:24:11.910 --> 00:24:13.789
As you move on here, this gives

00:24:13.790 --> 00:24:15.829
the patient view of the pain

00:24:15.830 --> 00:24:18.769
of all the

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:20.769
little things that happen during

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:22.029
an admission, a lab result, an

00:24:22.030 --> 00:24:24.569
imaging result, diagnostics,

00:24:24.570 --> 00:24:26.669
infusions, nursing orders,

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:27.829
a procedure.

00:24:27.830 --> 00:24:29.549
And imagine being able, the dream

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.449
is, if we reach such a

00:24:31.450 --> 00:24:33.369
high level of automation, could

00:24:33.370 --> 00:24:35.349
we get to the point where we have a

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:36.649
digital screen at the foot of the

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:38.709
